##
    Building a thing in the wrong order.

    This program does not compile. Every mistake here is the same one told three ways: something
    is read before whatever decides its value has run.

    A parent settles its own state before a child adds to it, so 'base(...)' comes first and a
    child that cannot reach a parent's constructor has to say so. A field's starting value runs
    before any constructor at all, so nothing there can look at the instance it belongs to.

    All three are also C#'s rules. That is the point of them being rules here: what a reader
    learns about construction in Profi-C is what they will find waiting for them.
##

model Person

    public string name;

    public function Person(string given)
        this.name = given;
    end function

end model

model Employee extends Person

    public string title;

    public function Employee(string given, string role)
        # Wrong: the parent has to be built before anything reads what it holds. Written here,
        # this line sees 'name' still empty, because Person's constructor has not run yet.
        Console.WriteLine("building " + this.name);

        base(given);

        this.title = role;
    end function

end model

model Volunteer extends Person

    # Wrong: Person cannot be built without being given a name, and nothing here gives it one.
    # Without the rule this compiles and runs, and every Volunteer has an empty name — which
    # looks like a mistake in whatever printed it rather than one here.
    public function Volunteer()
        Console.WriteLine("a volunteer");
    end function

end model

model Rectangle

    public integer width = 4;

    # Wrong: nothing is built yet. 'width' above has run, but only because it was written first
    # — reorder the two lines and this reads zero instead, with nothing anywhere reporting it.
    # A value that depends on another belongs in a constructor.
    public integer area = this.width * 3;

end model

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Employee worker = new Employee("Ada", "engineer");
        Volunteer helper = new Volunteer();
        Rectangle shape = new Rectangle();

        Console.WriteLine(worker.title + " " + helper.name + " " + shape.area);
    end function

end model
